<section xml:id="sec-mod4-assessment">
  <title>Assessment</title>
  <p>Estimated time: 45 minutes.</p>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <p>
      This assessment has three parts. Parts 1 and 2 ask you to write
      complete mathematical proofs, submitted for feedback. Part 3 is
      a short reflection. Submit all three parts together as a single
      document (PDF or typed file) through the course management system.
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Write-up standard.</alert> All proofs must be written in
      complete mathematical prose. Use the style modeled in the Worked
      Examples: include scratch work (clearly labeled) before the formal
      proof, announce your strategy, write in complete grammatical
      sentences, and justify every step. Work consisting only of symbolic
      manipulations without explanation will not receive full credit.
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>A note on Part 2.</alert> All students complete all three
      parts. Part 2 offers two options. Option A asks you to evaluate a
      limit with the Algebra of Limits. Option B asks for a proof,
      directly from the definitions, that every convergent sequence is
      Cauchy, and is the more demanding. Choose the option that will
      stretch you.
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Academic integrity.</alert> Your proofs must be your own
      work. You may refer to your notes, the textbooks, and the worked
      examples from this module. You may not collaborate with other
      students on the written proofs or use AI writing tools to draft
      your arguments.
    </p>
  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod4-as-part1">
    <title>Part 1: Core Proof (Required)</title>
    <p>
      Estimated time: 25 minutes.
    </p>
    <p>
      Consider the sequence defined by
      <md>a_n = \frac{4n + 7}{2n + 5}.</md>
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod4-as-1a">
    <title>Conjecturing the Limit</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Based on the Algebra of Limits (or on straightforward algebra
        on the terms), identify the value of <m>\displaystyle\lim_{n \to \infty} a_n</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Show your algebra clearly. This work does not need to be in
        formal proof style; it is the analysis step that precedes the
        <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>N</m> proof.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Divide the numerator and the denominator by the highest power
        of <m>n</m> that appears, and ask what each resulting term does
        as <m>n</m> grows.
      </p>
    </hint>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod4-as-1b">
    <title>An <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>N</m> Proof of the Limit</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Using your work in the previous problem, prove directly from
        the <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>N</m> definition that
        <md>a_n = \frac{4n + 7}{2n + 5} \longrightarrow 2.</md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Your proof must have the following structure:
        <ol marker="(i)">
          <li>
            <em>Scratch work (clearly labeled).</em> Simplify
            <m>|a_n - 2|</m> by combining over a common denominator.
            Determine the condition on <m>n</m> under which
            <m>|a_n - 2| \lt \varepsilon</m>, and describe how you
            will choose <m>N</m>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <em>Formal proof.</em> Write a complete proof beginning
            <q>Let <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>.</q> Produce <m>N \in \N</m>
            (invoking the Archimedean Property by name), and verify
            that <m>n \geq N</m> implies <m>|a_n - 2| \lt \varepsilon</m>,
            with every inequality in the verification labeled with
            its justification.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        After you combine <m>a_n - 2</m> over a common denominator,
        you may solve the inequality for <m>n</m> exactly, or first
        replace the denominator by a smaller, simpler expression in
        <m>n</m>. The second route gives a larger but simpler upper
        bound for <m>|a_n - 2|</m>, and an <m>N</m> that is easier to
        write down.
      </p>
    </hint>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod4-as-1c">
    <title>The Role of Completeness</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        In one or two sentences, explain where the Completeness Axiom
        enters your proof in the previous problem. (Hint: does it
        enter directly, or through the Archimedean Property?)
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod4-as-part2">
    <title>Part 2: Proof Choice (Required; Choose One)</title>
    <p>
      Estimated time: 15 minutes.
    </p>
    <p>
      Choose <em>one</em> of the following two proof problems.
      Both ask for a complete proof; they differ in difficulty and
      the techniques required. Label your submission clearly with
      <q>Option A</q> or <q>Option B.</q>
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod4-as-2a">
    <title>Option A (Standard): A Limit via the Algebra of Limits</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Evaluate
        <md>\lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{3n^2 - n}{n^2 + 4}.</md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Write a complete solution using the Algebra of Limits
        (Zorn's Theorem 2.5) and the known limit <m>1/n \to 0</m>. Do not
        write an <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>N</m> proof.
      </p>
      <p>
        Your solution must:
        (i) begin with an algebraic rewrite of the expression that
        puts every term in a form whose limit you know,
        (ii) evaluate the numerator and denominator limits separately,
        stating which limit law you invoke at each step, and
        (iii) verify the nonvanishing hypothesis of the quotient rule
        before applying it.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Divide numerator and denominator by <m>n^2</m>. The limit
        law on quotients requires the denominator's limit to be nonzero;
        confirm this before taking the quotient.
      </p>
    </hint>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod4-as-2b">
    <title>Option B (More Challenging): Convergent Implies Cauchy</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove directly from the definitions that every convergent
        sequence of real numbers is Cauchy.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is: suppose <m>(a_n)</m> is a sequence with <m>a_n \to L</m>.
        Prove that for every <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>, there exists
        <m>N \in \N</m> such that <m>m, n \geq N</m> implies
        <m>|a_m - a_n| \lt \varepsilon</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Your proof must clearly identify: what you assume from the
        convergence hypothesis, how you split <m>\varepsilon</m>,
        and where the Triangle Inequality is applied. Your proof
        should not use any theorem beyond the
        <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>N</m> definition and the Triangle
        Inequality.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        The convergence hypothesis controls how far each term is from
        <m>L</m>, but the goal concerns the distance between two
        terms. Rewrite <m>a_m - a_n</m> so that <m>L</m> appears, then
        decide what tolerance to request from the convergence
        definition so that your two estimates add up to less than
        <m>\varepsilon</m>.
      </p>
    </hint>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod4-as-part3">
    <title>Part 3: Reflection</title>
    <p>
      Estimated time: 5 minutes.
    </p>
    <p>
      Write 3<ndash/>5 sentences for each question. These are graded
      for thoughtfulness, not for mathematical correctness.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod4-as-r1">
    <title>Quantifier Alternation</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        In <xref ref="ex-mod4-as-1b"/>, where did you make
        a choice that <em>depended on</em> <m>\varepsilon</m>? What
        would have broken in the proof if your choice of <m>N</m>
        had been made before seeing <m>\varepsilon</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod4-as-r2">
    <title>Bauldry's Presentation</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        In the Bridge Reading you read Bauldry's treatment of
        sequences (&#xa7;2.5). Compare the presentation style to
        Zorn's: what is more compressed in Bauldry, and what feels
        more like the Zorn text you worked through in the study guide?
        If there is a single Bauldry sentence that you had to read
        more than once to understand, quote it and describe what was
        implicit.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod4-as-r3">
    <title>Looking Ahead</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        In <xref ref="ch-mod6"/>, you will prove that a function <m>f</m> is
        continuous at <m>a</m> using an
        <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>\delta</m> argument: for every
        <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m> there exists <m>\delta \gt 0</m>
        such that <m>|x - a| \lt \delta</m> implies
        <m>|f(x) - f(a)| \lt \varepsilon</m>. Based on what you
        practiced in this module, describe the connection you see
        between the <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>N</m> structure of a
        sequence convergence proof and the
        <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>\delta</m> structure of a continuity
        proof. What is similar? What role does <m>N</m> play that
        <m>\delta</m> will play?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod4-as-rubric">
    <title>Assessment Rubric (Informational)</title>
    <p>
      The following rubric describes how submitted proofs will be
      evaluated.
    </p>
    <tabular halign="left">
      <col width="14%"/>
      <col width="29%"/>
      <col width="29%"/>
      <col width="28%"/>
      <row header="yes">
        <cell><p>Criterion</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Full credit</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Partial credit</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Minimal credit</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p><alert>Logical correctness</alert></p></cell>
        <cell><p>All steps are valid; the argument proves exactly the stated claim</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Mostly correct with one fixable gap</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Significant logical errors or wrong claim proved</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p><alert>Use of definitions</alert></p></cell>
        <cell><p>The <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>N</m> definition is invoked correctly; quantifier order is explicit</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Definition invoked but quantifier dependency obscured</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Definition not invoked or confused with a different property</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p><alert>Choice of <m>N</m></alert></p></cell>
        <cell><p>An explicit <m>N</m> depending on <m>\varepsilon</m> is produced and shown to work</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Correct idea but the verification that <m>N</m> works is incomplete</p></cell>
        <cell><p>No explicit <m>N</m>, or <m>N</m> chosen without reference to <m>\varepsilon</m></p></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p><alert>Mathematical prose</alert></p></cell>
        <cell><p>Complete grammatical sentences; scratch work labeled and separate from proof</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Mostly prose with some missing connective sentences</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Primarily symbolic with no connecting narrative</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p><alert>Reflection</alert></p></cell>
        <cell><p>Specific, thoughtful, and engages with the mathematical content</p></cell>
        <cell><p>General but relevant observations</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Vague or too brief to be informative</p></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </paragraphs>

</section>
